#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "260712d9-b48d-552e-a31a-0c2e74cafeb0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6241" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2] \\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/4f/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/0a/0ad490af48e1db93ab9cae7e482b5eb8956aa14f8d2972816f8852179b35c4cb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/a3/a33927647d7651ce47c9e6619bd26a29ceb5cc5dd74e379bf81ea61806fb5c95.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=2+3+4+5=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151937 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "07269d0a-faf3-5bf9-b397-8fc1574313a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6242" :question/problem "Let $R = [1,3] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/3b/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa44875a37e8c7b7bc69b175ac1f30fe73af936565e68ecaa82e02b7617b330.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/59/595d13ca8ec18056038a855e96ca4a3d8cc967182dae5c54b9746c731238413b.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,1)=15 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,1)=10 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,2)=12 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,2)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = 15+10+12+7 = 44.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152041 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/id #uuid "60ec2df0-87b9-544f-b286-2ed5e17d92fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9476" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8de3a1512666201ab8d9ab7c06385dac08acb67345273a2e07252616f666cf27.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Express the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL = m_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22} \\\\  & =3{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3800ea81-5813-5881-9876-a8523b318270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}-{m}_{21}-{m}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565020} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc140b2-ecc8-5327-bddd-61b81b9f4427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{31})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565050} {:answer/id #uuid "a52d19ad-294e-5ebe-afc8-bb1ccf084e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565190} {:answer/id #uuid "f11656ee-b6cc-56d4-b249-32402c686bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565300} {:answer/id #uuid "faf1dc4d-ba4d-5d53-a2f0-fd3508c89b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}\\cdot {m}_{21}\\cdot {m}_{31}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fc140b2-ecc8-5327-bddd-61b81b9f4427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{31})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565050} :answer-field/id #uuid "61541d20-7c76-5499-b8e4-4955bf8e417c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfe6201a-0cdf-54aa-a233-33c1bb74e932" :question/math-academy-id "q-122873" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c6ed4ab6e979f6df3cba0b2afc442dec11667b7b81027cd692e01f1106ee53fa.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{31})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\cdot {m}_{11}+2\\cdot {m}_{21}+2\\cdot {m}_{31} \\\\  & =2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{31}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12704151-2a3f-5c78-82a6-3698cf12fb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540559} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4984a1-bf54-50db-b28e-4424d035c287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540581} {:answer/id #uuid "81e1aec9-02c9-5d64-aa88-79ce6c1d9935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}-{m}_{21}-{m}_{12}-{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540746} {:answer/id #uuid "b62b1dcc-a7a2-515a-82fc-67a62e3f0afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}-{m}_{12}-{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540832} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0995e6-6731-5004-bd04-b2911f5c2cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}\\cdot {m}_{21}\\cdot {m}_{12}\\cdot {m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f4984a1-bf54-50db-b28e-4424d035c287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540581} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee86ad7-df28-501c-a81c-6bd872b01f2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f89ca459-c179-53fe-83c0-45ad6670695a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122874" :question/problem "![](images/03/03935d4a02a1a42a106e148fa120132a246818684f88a441f63de46bc01dcbdc.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =1\\cdot {m}_{11}+1\\cdot {m}_{21}+1\\cdot {m}_{12}+1\\cdot {m}_{22} \\\\  & ={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d188a23-dab3-5b6e-8263-d8a3e3585db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540552} {:answer/id #uuid "8937b8f1-b84c-52e8-97eb-b8be5b9c9cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+3{m}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540760} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7801d-9b07-56e2-a485-354d609cc6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540845} {:answer/id #uuid "c84f6a96-35e9-5f82-ba6e-69dc607bc73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540863} {:answer/id #uuid "d8551ee9-261d-5834-b7b2-72906db68e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d188a23-dab3-5b6e-8263-d8a3e3585db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540552} :answer-field/id #uuid "366ce63e-4758-55ab-ade5-2bbdeee37bb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfbdcb48-ce24-5aa4-b673-ed006db801e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122885" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b00e13fc7b827b9c3960073679ee651f6bce5a6193e0b0af1907738691a287ed.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=1,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =1\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{21}+2\\cdot {m}_{12} \\\\  & ={m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d6fd7a-77ba-50da-9b48-5f42b4a3f71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540529} {:answer/id #uuid "0b1831a8-7319-5cf3-9494-86fd997692f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540545} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdf7f22-f9ed-5577-a8be-e560b2430cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540627} {:answer/id #uuid "9f49662e-9f1d-5dac-a394-6f82ad2e70e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540794} {:answer/id #uuid "dabe35cb-066f-51f0-aee7-336adb36649f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bdf7f22-f9ed-5577-a8be-e560b2430cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540627} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb531dc-a984-5ad5-8e3d-3ead33a40597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cde06bdc-b497-5379-91e5-90582a7fb14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122886" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9544b511212f20620d516a6f14fcfa1d812a0119cc14f8741d113c6cb663961.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =1\\cdot {m}_{11}+1\\cdot {m}_{21}+2\\cdot {m}_{12}+2\\cdot {m}_{22} \\\\  & ={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ce993e2-3ca8-54ad-a607-312b53786484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565080} {:answer/id #uuid "5efcdd19-7c67-5031-a1b4-d680584b5e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+3{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565084} {:answer/id #uuid "7101590f-560d-56a0-92f5-866a481a1aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565108} {:answer/id #uuid "aeaf7430-4820-5029-9e60-633211b0422f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565198} {:answer/id #uuid "cac5a269-d1ef-595c-a5de-d2913ed48562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cac5a269-d1ef-595c-a5de-d2913ed48562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565243} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5b07574-165a-5003-a2ce-65d9f6400776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ca84db7-67f2-5f23-9873-fa001c3f6805" :question/math-academy-id "q-122951" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a418e8e8e84133cb672d312f5dde246876a0f261c97d739b62b7e5ced437764.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{13})=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13} \\\\  & ={m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0fa39e0-956a-5971-a717-a2b4ddbbbc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148908} {:answer/id #uuid "d0077eb1-34bb-5600-b1a2-be8c9f158d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148909} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c95708-1325-5ab4-8b83-59715ff43a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148910} {:answer/id #uuid "3553af4c-5978-53c6-badb-cb42ce8cf148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148911} {:answer/id #uuid "7eaedb76-9489-527b-ac20-15c077ad82b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0fa39e0-956a-5971-a717-a2b4ddbbbc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148908} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af36f43-964d-53de-b148-316a8c5425f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fa3d711-e06b-53b5-b006-e7a59f8870a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122960" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,3]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=6-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/17/178108b256e55eaa5bd58c72f606fbed0aef774ff10a46e059c70abe93f8b341.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/dd/dd521e876abfc4313afb5beaf568c1d93cad670cfa912e210ed5d1548c51ec97.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/05/0515af2764a6ac27b2b69998ea4b99079dd7266a5989a161d01a6d25a670678e.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,2)=2 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3f6271-26ac-5779-9fc0-4fb7ad90aa79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540622} {:answer/id #uuid "688c2410-3624-5fc3-b9cb-d5377915e7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540705} {:answer/id #uuid "6ade1e9f-48c8-5454-bff2-0de3aabc833c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540711} {:answer/id #uuid "888bac61-eb50-50bf-adde-8d2e2ddef8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540756} {:answer/id #uuid "fa76ed60-6738-528c-9aac-b75fcea7735d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ade1e9f-48c8-5454-bff2-0de3aabc833c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540711} :answer-field/id #uuid "14455e9e-ecaa-5111-b90d-483eb946543b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ad4ccbd-e338-5550-8a1d-a1b82832243e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122966" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=11-{x}^{2}-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/3b/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=11-{x}^{2}-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=11-{x}^{2}-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa44875a37e8c7b7bc69b175ac1f30fe73af936565e68ecaa82e02b7617b330.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/59/595d13ca8ec18056038a855e96ca4a3d8cc967182dae5c54b9746c731238413b.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,1)=6 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,1)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,2)=5 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,2)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=6+1+5+0=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135cdf1a-54bd-5516-b63f-c6dd787df5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253836} {:answer/id #uuid "14592777-13ea-5aa8-9a5d-9542c0a87417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253842} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc9310f-08d7-54cb-9fe4-e39e036154cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255346} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb5c86d-bcba-5c4b-8e15-19a38ace784c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255618} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a3e07c-83c6-5375-8b22-6bd840c4d6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbc9310f-08d7-54cb-9fe4-e39e036154cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255346} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b8abff1-1635-5a0e-87c3-9c866bf145ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aea78735-0849-5195-bf1a-fcbc0a64996f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122982" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,4]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}+2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/c3/c30acb7cb94886e612675d5e64838961134e8d09e5d88c1d803067832f4b173e.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}+2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}+2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/e5/e5c92768512480b16fcca735126b303c4ca372d22a5c735fc8cea1588a3c5811.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/b0/b015f94b135ca669c0711057c7245982eb8f0257e4947a603571f2826364b8fa.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,0)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,0)=4 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,0)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84cea8f0-fe19-5409-aa0a-da3df7628c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148914} {:answer/id #uuid "afc7881c-53fc-57cb-8c3d-4a4ae4791a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148915} {:answer/id #uuid "09063c2b-16dd-523d-a7d4-61f1a4c2c8f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148916} {:answer/id #uuid "0f9768ea-9823-5b7d-830e-000049b21701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148917} {:answer/id #uuid "442a0d7b-02ad-5695-bab7-55c4b3aead9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "442a0d7b-02ad-5695-bab7-55c4b3aead9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148918} :answer-field/id #uuid "698f0c52-51d8-50e3-bc12-87a9cadb2925" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "062ef86d-a8df-5cf2-9d44-780a221bd229" :question/math-academy-id "q-122985" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2x+3y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff5612495e4f64e710963ca4d4fc6b7b259164c7ef1943aa37467a71c1d48353.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+3y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+3y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa7288d56b6604cf0ad013941003642209cf8ae75eca22d791cc5b65df4de35.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/be/be946433e503c6d7125fd53de795b5606666f20f3aa6164dd7e7b947b08af6d6.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,0)=5 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(0,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,1)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57479917-67d8-5013-96bf-d03d0cec796d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565071} {:answer/id #uuid "69c6207d-8d80-5cde-8944-57ceb4aef0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565099} {:answer/id #uuid "8c966508-5722-5ab1-b2e6-984f8b68946d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565155} {:answer/id #uuid "996732db-d52d-5214-965f-c7db143a083d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565171} {:answer/id #uuid "d83a61cf-5027-53d7-a7f2-fe2053a5b5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996732db-d52d-5214-965f-c7db143a083d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565171} :answer-field/id #uuid "b76b9c41-1ad3-5f84-b769-5d10b8aa414c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38c907cd-2c27-580b-8563-70cc82b39b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75038" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=4-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/3c/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=4-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=4-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/0f/0fefe892c74e8d391e56ee822f83e7e0fe0110593c9d6a4b93b8cfe1e558ab16.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/fe/fe872f1f1b0307760dcdba6ac6f6be20f197662ac250ab46dc77fa472295906f.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=1 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2+1+1+0=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107dfb62-1b9d-51c8-9744-e1f29dd07655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540558} {:answer/id #uuid "88b3a027-2fb3-5589-8df2-d6ac588524bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540757} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8d42e0-eaa9-505b-8931-064524f4bd68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540817} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6dcd45-400c-5812-8da7-5f3fb8564611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540839} {:answer/id #uuid "f58f6e34-2e0f-5f5d-832d-5b3725016996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac8d42e0-eaa9-505b-8931-064524f4bd68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540817} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a4ee6d-5997-53cb-a03a-d8841c187de9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73bd1c38-9ed6-5451-94fd-2aa984462a1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75039" :question/problem "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/a3/a30d43569026cdf8f4ce960016620a725248726523abd005dbad5bc903123b9f.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/a8/a81396ee6d33b1ac10a44b6b8db2ded2612e86274e017ebe95c55420b7f73265.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c9/c90a6e6d4c9e9a92bfd1c42b8350941b7d45f96d422e2a1988a4c003df6457e1.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,2)=8 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,2)=13 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,3)=13 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,3)=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =8+13+13+18 \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0432d4eb-63b0-56eb-bf7b-7e0a2409ea51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564947} {:answer/id #uuid "1b377a21-5851-5233-ba67-1bd25d4c8f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564979} {:answer/id #uuid "9c26311a-5f11-56c5-ade9-4c523fc7c350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565175} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ee0dad-3131-5b08-b7fc-b7b8b68f9a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565278} {:answer/id #uuid "ea56ea57-0f71-519e-a988-f5b5c4c2111a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea56ea57-0f71-519e-a988-f5b5c4c2111a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565287} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f9c92a2-2c2c-5f88-a31b-706d15e85fb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aebb283d-a6dd-5621-b2da-04fb6f0be9f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-75231" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=3x+y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2c33fe367e36506f949f1f76d84ccb2b3ff74ea3a4456e57982f489978225b3.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $2$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=3x+y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=3x+y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/3b/3bdc77445e75c7c74d6e242f9eb469687aa34384e7399b05607dfaa4a81ae596.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/ac/acded82358333752ba407673edf543262ee0b36166014c3de54d9beef9218a26.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,1)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3951a8-dde7-5b56-bc08-28b7fbe0bd99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564963} {:answer/id #uuid "887d8243-ec17-5a15-b60c-9d35f62cb56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565150} {:answer/id #uuid "abbcdeef-5cb8-52d6-b2e7-3318843356b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565193} {:answer/id #uuid "ba710b16-4bd3-5083-a30b-2dbd0e05b4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565221} {:answer/id #uuid "c7084f82-22f4-55a8-a979-f37698ccd1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abbcdeef-5cb8-52d6-b2e7-3318843356b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565193} :answer-field/id #uuid "352dbe2b-1ccb-5161-9599-03540de2d249" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "559bc70c-78ac-5c05-9067-d915f9f4b231" :question/math-academy-id "q-75235" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [0,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/29/293e79685a3f4da205d986bcf4331e4b4f43c0a48fe183d27f93aaee6bb17b99.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4{m}_{11}+4{m}_{21}+4{m}_{12}+4{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/12/12df172e3a2f60a8b123937600d5774508ac8cb204696414690490be8d9e2c32.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/b5/b5e72e970d512740cc0d5de9e2d5739d71fb0c90e750c9874042ff4fa5230db9.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,0)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =4\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "258f88c7-c0a2-56e8-9230-059a60c0a8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551751} {:answer/id #uuid "858f5818-4647-5518-b75d-f2e9dd90e7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551985} {:answer/id #uuid "98578764-7601-57dc-9d67-e947a3ce724a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552034} {:answer/id #uuid "b17bf7f2-0d3c-57ff-a579-7f0faaabe3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552094} {:answer/id #uuid "ec56fda9-5a7a-576c-a3c2-f64506f5aa01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "258f88c7-c0a2-56e8-9230-059a60c0a8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551751} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7df747d-5ae6-580b-89b5-66c235ad174c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a55a479e-4a45-5971-8018-f156cd1ee741" :question/math-academy-id "q-75252" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [1,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=9-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,3,5\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/97/97220aa0d23d01e807608e6194a37661ba77cc391d0adf537908c1f13b1b6de8.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4{m}_{11}+4{m}_{21}+4{m}_{12}+4{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=9-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=9-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/30/306666a0e69fca9dc47c2295aa616a944482f00b28fdd96d9d96af816b058447.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/15/15512c5f5063309474534a54b323d87c28cf9b776c10789a8f70e12cdb898aeb.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,3)=4 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(4,3)=2 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,5)=2 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(4,5)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 0=32.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd184c5-a1ab-5ea2-a565-dc9e11d0577f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540551} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9bb78a-6144-5f51-9960-0c3b78126ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540578} {:answer/id #uuid "47051153-0c3e-51ec-b6c0-503c70ae170b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540652} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a25326-be2d-5c80-8dde-5f079287a42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540813} {:answer/id #uuid "b81ae7bf-dac2-515c-8794-1cd63313830e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cd184c5-a1ab-5ea2-a565-dc9e11d0577f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540551} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c51992-c8f6-553a-ab49-de91cc2fad16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68f740c4-3eaf-53af-86af-a7b05d82500b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75253" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [0,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=20-2x-3y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/29/293e79685a3f4da205d986bcf4331e4b4f43c0a48fe183d27f93aaee6bb17b99.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4{m}_{11}+4{m}_{21}+4{m}_{12}+4{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=20-2x-3y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=20-2x-3y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/cc/cc6e04660f00d04307715159c25aa2860ca08af74ed36fc9b0be15d10ee94a27.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/74/744aeb24bb4fd4487b35ffd789d957879cb0c3ee92e40519f71b65ee5ee76dcf.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,2)=10 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(4,2)=6 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,4)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(4,4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4\\cdot 10+4\\cdot 6+4\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 0=80.\n$$"}]}
